---
/**
 * The theme override control (#92): an editorial chip cycling
 * system -> light/dark override -> back to system. All state semantics,
 * persistence, and the after-swap re-stamp live in src/scripts/theme.ts; the
 * pre-paint gate in BaseLayout stamps html[data-theme] so a stored override
 * never flashes.
 *
 * Placement (#101): mounted from SiteFooter as a normal in-flow child of the
 * footer row (copyright left, control right), NOT from BaseLayout as a fixed
 * overlay. The fixed version was a mobile defect: the content column nearly
 * fills a 390px viewport, so an opaque chip pinned to the viewport sat over
 * live body text for the entire scroll, not just at the page bottom. In flow
 * it cannot overlap anything, so the whole class of bug is gone rather than
 * mitigated. Cost accepted in #101: the footer is below the fold, which is
 * less discoverable than top chrome, but the site has no shared site-wide
 * header to dock into.
 *
 * The footer (and so this control) renders on every page, /chat included
 * since B2 Phase 2 (#108): the chat page sits on the editorial ground now,
 * so the control has real work there; only the slab interior stays
 * always-night.
 *
 * This component carries markup + style only, no <script>: theme.ts is
 * imported by BaseLayout's single hydration point alongside terminal.ts, per
 * the one-deferred-module contract in that file. theme.ts delegates its click
 * handler on document against #themectl, so the node's position in the DOM is
 * irrelevant to it.
 *
 * The visible label always reflects the current state with zero JS: all
 * three labels are server-rendered and CSS keyed on html[data-theme] shows
 * exactly one. display:none drops the hidden two from the accessible name
 * as well, so the button's name ("theme: system" / "theme: light" /
 * "theme: dark") is correct from first paint, before any script runs, and
 * speech-input users can say exactly what they see.
 *
 * Material: the control recolors the PAGE, so it sits on the page ground in
 * the bracket-control idiom; it is never phosphor (#67: phosphor is the
 * machine's material, and the machine ignores this control). The hover fill
 * comes from the unified bracket rule in terminal.css, shared with .cbtn,
 * so the two can never drift.
 *
 * Copy: bracket-label tokens stay lowercase (#76); no dashes (#18); never
 * the t-word in visitor-facing copy (#58).
 */
---

<button type="button" class="themectl" id="themectl">
  <span class="br" aria-hidden="true">[</span><span class="lbl"
    ><span class="tlab tlab-system">theme: system</span><span
      class="tlab tlab-light">theme: light</span
    ><span class="tlab tlab-dark">theme: dark</span></span
  ><span class="br" aria-hidden="true">]</span>
</button>

<style>
  /* In flow in the footer row: no position, no z-index, no opaque background
     to hide anything behind (#101). The border keeps it reading as a control
     rather than as more footer text, which is what the copyright beside it
     already is. */
  .themectl {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 10px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 7px;
    background: none;
    color: var(--dim); /* the brackets */
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .lbl {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* one visible label, keyed on the pre-paint html[data-theme] stamp */
  .tlab {
    display: none;
  }
  :global(html:not([data-theme])) .tlab-system,
  :global(html[data-theme="light"]) .tlab-light,
  :global(html[data-theme="dark"]) .tlab-dark {
    display: inline;
  }
</style>
